<TopicPage slug="subjects/political-science/intermediate/international-relations-theories">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    International Relations (IR) asks <strong>why states (and others) do what they do on the world
    stage</strong> — war, trade, treaties, institutions. Three grand theories dominate:
    realism (power), liberalism (institutions) and constructivism (ideas) — each explains some
    events brilliantly and others badly, which is why professionals carry all three.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the three grand theories and the critics" widget="equationStep" config={irCfg} />

  <h2>Concepts the theories fight over</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The security dilemma:</strong> defending yourself looks like preparing to attack — arms race logic without anyone wanting war (the game-theory lesson's prisoner's dilemma, at planetary scale).</li>
    <li><strong>Balance of power vs hegemony:</strong> do states gang up against the strongest (balancing) or join them (bandwagoning)? The answer shapes every prediction about rising powers.</li>
    <li><strong>Nuclear weapons changed the game:</strong> great-power war between nuclear states hasn't happened (75+ years) — deterrence's strongest evidence, and IR's biggest open question is whether it holds forever.</li>
    <li><strong>Non-state actors complicate the picture:</strong> corporations (bigger economies than states), NGOs, terrorists, climate — the state-centric theories keep stretching to fit.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Kant (1795); Morgenthau, Politics Among Nations (1948); Wendt, "Anarchy Is What States Make of It" (1992); Russett on democratic peace.</p>
</TopicPage>
